lt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxmd265\" style=\"width: 193px; height:auto;max-width:100%;display:block;background:#fff;\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.Quantrex Academy.app/pyq/jee_main/68f55948-9bd0-4fc5-9c11-7029131f33b3-image.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F68f55948-9bd0-4fc5-9c11-7029131f33b3-image.png?alt=media\" onerror=\"if(window.QxOwnedFigs&&QxOwnedFigs.retryOnError)QxOwnedFigs.retryOnError(this)\" class=\"qx-pool-fig qx-no-wm qx-sol-fig\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"eager\"><br /><br />In the acidic medium, only basic nitrogen is protonated. The second nitrogen is less basic because the lone pair is involved in aromaticity, while $1$ and $3$ are more basic.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login_id","_marksId":"648b5a67417cc3fb48d66940","question":"The correct structure of histidine in a strongly acidic solution $\\left(pH = 2\\right)$ is","explanation":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252F68f55948-9bd0-4fc5-9c11-7029131f33b3-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxmd265\" style=\"width: 193px; height:auto;max-width:100%;display:block;background:#fff;\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.Quantrex Academy.app/pyq/jee_main/68f55948-9bd0-4fc5-9c11-7029131f33b3-image.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F68f55948-9bd0-4fc5-9c11-7029131f33b3-image.png?alt=media\" onerror=\"if(window.QxOwnedFigs&&QxOwnedFigs.retryOnError)QxOwnedFigs.retryOnError(this)\" class=\"qx-pool-fig qx-no-wm qx-sol-fig\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"eager\"><br /><br />In the acidic medium, only basic nitrogen is protonated. The second nitrogen is less basic because the lone pair is involved in aromaticity, while $1$ and $3$ are more basic.</p>"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f1a","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f1a","q":"<p>Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).</p><p><strong>Assertion (A):</strong> Ketoses give Seliwanoff’s test faster than Aldoses.</p><p><strong>Reason (R):</strong> Ketoses undergo $\\beta$-elimination followed by formation of furfural.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>","options":["(A) is false but (R) is true","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Seliwanoff 's test is a differentiating test for Ketose and aldose. This test relies on the principle that the keto hexose are more rapidly dehydrated to form $5$-hydroxy methyl furfural when heated in acidic medium which on condensation with resorcinol, Cherry red or brown red coloured complex is formed rapidly indicating a positive test.& </p><p>Aldoses take longer time to produce colour because under the same conditions, aldoses form furfural slowly, probably because &beta;-elimination is required before dehydration to furfural.& Therefore prolonged heating should be avoided.</p><p>Hence, Assertion is true but& Reason is false.& </p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f1f","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f1f","q":"Which one among the following chemical tests is used to distinguish monosaccharide from disaccharide ?","options":["Seliwanoff's test","Iodine test","Barfoed test","Tollen's test"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Barford test is used for distinguish monosaccharide from disaccharide","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f24","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f24","q":"Identify the correct statement among the following:","options":["All naturally occurring amino acids except glycine contain one chiral centre.","All naturally occurring amino acids are optically active.","Glutamic acid is the only amino acid that contains a-COOH group at the side chain.","Amino acid, cysteine easily undergo dimerization due to the presence of free SH group."],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"* Isoleucine has 2 chiral centre<br />* Glycine is optically inactive<br />* Aspartic acid also contain COOH group at the side chain.<br />* Cysteine easily dimmerise due to free SH group","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f29","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f29","q":"Out of the following, which type of interaction is responsible for the stabilisation of $\\alpha -$helix structure of proteins?","options":["Covalent bonding","Ionic bonding","Hydrogen bonding","vander Waals forces"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Hydrogen bonding is responsible for the stacking of $\\alpha -$helix structure of protein.</p><p>The $\\alpha$-helix structure of proteins is stabilized& by intramolecular H-bonding between C−O of one amino acid residue and the N−H of the fourth amino acid residue in the chain.& </p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f2e","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f2e","q":"Compound A gives D-Galactose and D-Glucose on hydrolysis. The compound A& is :","options":["Amylose","Sucrose","Maltose","Lactose"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"- Maltose is composed of two glucose units.\n- Sucrose has glycosidic linkage between $\\mathrm{C}_1$ of $\\alpha$-glucose and $C_2$ of $\\beta$-fructose.\n- Lactose is composed of galactose and glucose\n- Amylose has glycosidic linkage between $C_1$ of $\\alpha$-glucose $C_4$ of $\\alpha$-glucose.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f33","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f33","q":"Which one of the following tests used for the identification of functional groups in organic compounds does not use copper reagent?","options":["Seliwanoff's test","Biuret test for peptide bond","Barfoed's& test","Benedict's test"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Biomolecules","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Seliwanoff's Test : This test is used to detect the presence of Ketohexose.<br />Reagent : Resorcinol $+$ conc. $HCl$.<br />Barfoed's Test : It is~used to detect Monosaccharide by reduction of $Cu ( II )$ into Copper (I). Fehling Solution: It is used to detect aldehyde by reduction $Cu ( II )$ into $Cu ( I )$. Biuret~Test : Use to detect presence of peptide bond in protein by reduction of $Cu ( II )$ into $Cu ( I )$.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21fb7e39d99b57bc5baf"},{"id":"qz_69de4f822ee0e063d9292f38","_quizrrId":"69de4f822ee0e063d9292f38","q":"Which of the following is correct structure of tyrosine?","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-cf12307a1509e0e7.png?alt=me